This is a C++23 project that converts video files into 1-bit dithered video using Sierra error diffusion dithering. It depends on OpenCV 4.10+.

Build: cd build && cmake .. && make
Run: ./build/video-dither -h 480 -w 640 input.avi output.avi

Source layout:
- src/main.cpp — entry point, calls parseArguments() then processFramesConcurrently()
- src/argparse.cpp/hpp — CLI argument parsing (short and long flag formats)
- src/video.cpp/hpp — Sierra dithering algorithm and frame processing
- src/concurrency.cpp/hpp — producer-consumer threading pipeline with worker pool
- CMakeLists.txt — build config (C++23, OpenCV linkage)

Architecture: Three-stage concurrent pipeline (producer → worker pool → consumer). Producer reads frames, workers dither in parallel, consumer writes in order via priority queue. Uses binary semaphores, condition variables, and atomic flags for synchronization.

Key conventions:
- Use C++23 features and standard library threading primitives
- Follow existing code style (no namespaces, free functions, global synchronization primitives in concurrency module)
- OpenCV for all video I/O and image manipulation
- Output format is Motion JPEG, audio is discarded
